@@s001 Sequence alignment: edit distance
@@s002 ora:cell I compute,ok:answer,dato:base cases,due:where the minimum comes from
@@s003 Edit distance between <b>ALTO</b> and <b>ALTRO</b>: the minimum of insertions, deletions and substitutions. <code>OPT(i, j)</code> = distance between the first i characters of A and the first j of B.
@@s004 Base cases: <code>OPT(i, 0) = i</code> and <code>OPT(0, j) = j</code>: first row and first column. Already filled.
@@s005 <code>OPT(i, j) = min( OPT(i−1, j) + 1, OPT(i, j−1) + 1, OPT(i−1, j−1) + [characters different] )</code>. Cell (1,1): A[1]=A=B[1]: 0.
@@s006 Cell (4,4): ALTO against ALTR: the diagonal gives 1, the neighbour above 1+1, the one on the left 1+1. The minimum is 1: the row of the O is <b>1 1</b>.
@@s007 Answer: <b>OPT(4, 5) = 1</b>: the only difference is the <b>R</b> to insert. The cost is <code>O(n·m)</code>: a table of (n+1)(m+1) cells, each O(1).
@@s008 Sequence alignment: edit distance
@@s009 The edit distance between two strings counts the minimum number of insertions, deletions and substitutions. It is the DP on prefixes: a table of cells that depend on three neighbours.
@@s010 The problem and the recurrence
@@s011 <code>OPT(i, j)</code> is the distance between the first <code>i</code> characters of <code>A</code> and the first <code>j</code> of <code>B</code>. The base case is the zero row and column: <code>OPT(i, 0) = i</code>, <code>OPT(0, j) = j</code>. Then:
@@s012 <code>OPT(i, j) = min( OPT(i−1, j) + 1, OPT(i, j−1) + 1, OPT(i−1, j−1) + [A_i ≠ B_j] )</code>
@@s013 The three terms are: delete <code>A_i</code>, insert <code>B_j</code>, align the two characters (with substitution if they are different).
@@s014 The table is filled from left and from above
@@s015 The cost
@@s016 Table of <code>(n + 1)(m + 1)</code> cells, each in <code>O(1)</code>: time and space <code>O(nm)</code>. Reconstructing the alignment is done by climbing from the final cell towards the one that gave the minimum.
@@s017 The code, side by side
@@s018 This page explains the concept. The Java code that implements it, with a line-by-line explanation and the syntax notes needed, is on the side-by-side page.
